Burntime Remastered 1.3 for AROS
================================

Port release 1.

Burntime is Max Design's 1993 strategy game of survival after the bomb. This
is a port of Jakob Harder's Burntime Remastered, version 1.3, rewritten in
Rust for AROS. It plays the remaster's rules, Classic and Extended, for one
or two players against the Modern, Amiga or no machine opponents, in the
original graphics or the remaster's.

It contains none of the game's data. You need your own copy of Burntime.


1. What you need
----------------

- The archive for your system:
    aros-x86_64-abiv11-pc      AROS One and other x86_64 ABIv11 systems
    aros-x86_64-abiv1-pc       AROS mainline (ABIv1) on an x86_64 PC
    aros-aarch64-abiv1-raspi   AROS on the Raspberry Pi
  The three do not run on each other's systems.
- burntime.pack, made from your own copy of Burntime and the remaster's
  files with the packer (see 2.). The packer runs on another computer
  (macOS, Linux or Windows).
- Optionally the remaster's music (music/dos, music/amiga) and sounds.


2. Making burntime.pack
-----------------------

The packer is in the source archive of this release,
Burntime-1.3-aros1-source.zip. Its README.md says how to build it.
Lay your files out as burngfx/testdata/README.md in the source describes:
the remaster's 1.2 files, and in a v1.3 directory beside them the files
version 1.3 changed. Then:

    packer --from DIR --out burntime.pack --newgfx full

--newgfx full adds the remaster's graphics; the pack is then about 115 MB.
Without it the pack is about 21 MB and the game shows the original
graphics. A pack made from the 1.2 files alone is refused.


3. Installing
-------------

On x86_64, unpack the .zip archive where you want the game, or install the
.apkg.zip with apkg or PkgManager (arospkg, version 0.7.4 or later
recommended).

On the Raspberry Pi, AROS has no UnZip. Unpack the archive on another
computer and copy the Burntime drawer and Burntime.info onto the Pi's SD
card (its AROS partition). There is no package for the Pi (see 8.).

Put your data in a drawer of its own, outside the program's drawer, for
example Work:Burntime-Data, and tell the game where it is:

    MakeDir ENV:Burntime ENVARC:Burntime
    SetEnv SAVE Burntime/Data Work:Burntime-Data

The data drawer holds:

    burntime.pack
    music/dos/, music/amiga/     (optional)
    sounds/                      (optional)
    Saves/                       (made on the first save)

Without the variable the game looks in PROGDIR:Data. If it cannot read a
pack, it says which file it tried and why.


4. Running
----------

Start it from a Shell, with a large stack as it was tested:

    Stack 2000000
    Work:Burntime/Burntime

The window opens at half the screen, or larger where half the screen would
cut off the game. "Burntime window 800x600" opens it at that size. The
window can be resized; F11 or Alt+Enter switches to full screen.

How to play is in Manual, beside the program. The remaster's own manual
is in the game: MANUAL in the menus.


5. Controls and settings
------------------------

The mouse plays as in the original: left click to walk, enter, take and
choose; right click for the menu. The keyboard plays as the remaster does:

    arrows              walk the selected character, or move the selection
    W A S D             pan the view
    Tab, Shift+Tab      pick the next or previous target
    Space               act on the target
    Q                   attack, or travel on the map
    Shift+Left/Right    change the selected character
    Escape              open the menu, or close what is open
    T (held)            end the turn
    O H I R C           options, statistics, inventory, information,
                        fight or speak
    V or M              the world map
    F8                  the remaster's graphics or the original's
    F11, Alt+Enter      window or full screen

OPTIONS, then Settings, chooses the graphics, full screen, the input
prompts, the language, the music and the map music. The settings are kept
in ENV:Burntime/user.txt and ENVARC:Burntime/user.txt.


6. Saves, updating and removing
-------------------------------

Your data, saves and settings are not in the program's drawer. An update
replaces the program and its documents; removing it leaves your data. A
save loads again as long as the pack was made from the same game data.
Saves from builds before this release cannot be loaded; the game says so.
The remaster's saves cannot be loaded either.


7. Differences from Burntime Remastered 1.3
-------------------------------------------

- Saves are in this program's own format.
- The picture is drawn without the remaster's shaders (its no-shader look)
  and scaled to the window.
- The game advances in fixed steps of 1/18 second.
- The window opens larger than half the screen where half the screen would
  cut off the game.
- Where the remaster would stop with an error (building on the world map
  with a part missing, among others), this program goes on.
- On AROS mainline and the Raspberry Pi the game runs at a task priority
  one below normal (-1), because at the normal priority its sound stopped
  for seconds at a time. It sets the previous priority back when it ends.
- Touch controls and the remaster's experimental character pictures are
  not included.


8. Known limitations
--------------------

- On the Raspberry Pi, Avail FLUSH, or a program that asks for more memory
  than is free, makes AROS stop with a Software Failure (Illegal
  instruction, PC 0x8). This is an AROS defect in its USB controller
  driver (https://github.com/aros-development-team/AROS/issues/2068); a
  fix is proposed in
  https://github.com/aros-development-team/AROS/pull/2104. Do not use
  Avail FLUSH on the Pi. If it happens, restart AROS.

- On the Raspberry Pi, a Software Failure (Illegal instruction) has also
  appeared twice while the game was running, both times in QEMU's
  Raspberry Pi emulation after many games in one session. Its cause is not
  known. It shows the same signature as the defect above, but that does
  not show that it has the same cause, and the proposed fix for that
  defect is not known to prevent it. If it happens, restart AROS. Save
  your game often when you play on the Pi. About 137 MB of memory stay in
  use after the first game until the next restart.

- There is no package for the Raspberry Pi: a drawer that apkg installs on
  the Pi's system partition makes AROS ask for a volume "Burntime" when the
  game starts (an AROS FAT defect). Install by copying the drawer (see 3.).

- The game has not been tried on a physical Raspberry Pi, with sound on a
  Pi, or with a game controller.

- There is no AROS version of the packer.


